\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction LOOCV Validation}
\label{sec:loocv}

Leave-one-out cross-validation over 10 folds (each fold holds out one task's instructions) confirms the cross-instruction probe result is not driven by memorization.
Trained last\_preaction achieves $100\% \pm 0.00$ across all 10 folds at layers L1--L31, confirming perfect generalization.

\subsubsection{QA Probe: Progress and Subtask Classification}
\label{sec:qa_probe}

To test whether LLM layers encode task-relevant semantic information beyond instruction identity, we train probes to classify task progress (early/mid/late) and active subtask from frozen representations (Table~\ref{tab:qa_probe}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{QA probe accuracy (progress + subtask classification, best layer).}
\label{tab:qa_probe}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Source & Best Layer & Accuracy \\
\midrule
Trained last\_preaction & L20 & 86.3\% \\
Untrained last\_preaction & L20 & 85.3\% \\
DINOv2 cls\_token & --- & 82.7\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{RSA--$R^2$ peak offset.}
The $R^2$ plateau (L8--L16) encompasses and partially precedes the RSA peak (L12), indicating that maximal geometric alignment does not coincide with maximal decodability.
Training causes these peaks to converge on LIBERO-Goal (RSA L12, $R^2$ L13) but not on LIBERO-Spatial (6-layer gap), suggesting benchmark-specific alignment.

\paragraph{Implications for VLA design.}
Vision encoder pretraining quality is the primary design lever; investing in stronger self-supervised encoders may yield larger gains than scaling the LLM.
Representations from middle layers (L8--L13) at the readout token are preferable to the projector output or final layer, and instruction invariance ($|\Delta| < 0.006$) suggests instructions influence action selection through a mechanism other than modulating visual geometry~\cite{grant2026}.

